course: dictionary
lesson: 7293
title: Pirate, a word with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. Pirate can be a noun or a verb. «Jake was banned from after-school football for a term and not allowed in the school play, though secretly he wanted to be the pirate.» Here a pirate is a character in a play, a part Jake wanted, not a real robber at sea.
explain: Ask what the sentence is about. In a story about treasure and a ship, pirate means a sailor who robs ships. In a sentence about films or games, pirated means copied without permission. Then try your meaning in the sentence: does it still make sense?
explain: Pretend play uses words differently too. «When a bot pretends to be a pirate or a footballer, it is still pretending.» Saying «this sofa is a pirate ship» is pretend play, not a real ship.
fact: A pirate is a person who attacks and robs ships at sea.
fact: In stories and games, a pirate is a sailor with a ship, a flag and often hidden treasure.
fact: To pirate a film, song or game means to copy it or share it without permission and without paying.
fact: Pirates were real robbers of the sea in history, and piracy still happens in some parts of the world.
fact: Common phrases include «pirate ship», «pirate flag», «pirate treasure» and «dress up as a pirate».
exercise: In a story, what is a pirate? | A teacher who runs a school | A parrot that can talk | A type of fish that lives in the sea | *A sailor with a ship, a flag and often hidden treasure
exercise: Ella said she would pirate the new game from her friend. What does pirate mean here? | Dress up in an eye patch | *Copy it without permission and without paying | Share a sandwich at lunch | Sail the ship to find treasure
exercise: Jake wanted to be the pirate in the school play. What does pirate mean here? | *A character in a play | A copied film | A captain's chair on a ship | A robber of ships at sea
test: When a child says «this sofa is a pirate ship», what kind of play is it? | A museum visit | Copying a game | Real sea robbery | *Pretend play
test: Why is a pirated film a problem? | It is written in an old language | It has too many sea scenes | *It is copied without permission and without paying | It is too long to watch
exam: The word pirate can be a noun or a verb. In «Mia will pirate the song», which meaning fits? | *Copy and share it without permission | A flag with a skull on it | Play a part in a school show | A person who robs ships
